#: b1670f24864f45e3aa2d1b33f5888859
msgid "Instructions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:36
#: 65a8d2c059f1456db0d12b0a0c8ae9df
msgid "In the screenshot below, a Copter mission starts with an auto takeoff to 20 meters altitude; then goes to WP 2 rising to 100 meters altitude on the way, then waits 10 seconds; then the craft will proceed to WP 3 (descending to 50 meters altitude on the way), then returns to launch. After reaching the launch position, the craft will land. The mission assumes that the launch position is set at the home position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:47
#: 427b1b7228fa46bda7b3e12f60e5999d
msgid "Copter: Mission Planning Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:49
#: 3dcaea8925dd43f3b7630f8827f49092
msgid "You can enter waypoints and other commands (see the :ref:`Mission commands <common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events_mission_commands>` section below for more information). In the dropdown menus on each row, select the command you want. The column heading will change to show you what data that command requires. Lat and Lon can be entered by clicking on the map. Altitude is relative to your launch altitude/home position, so if you set 100m, for example, it will fly 100m above you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:58
#: 7e705c80f1b042159173c5b119b3821a
msgid "**Default Alt** is the default altitude when entering new waypoints. See :ref:`common-understanding-altitude` for altitude definitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:60
#: a8bc5c0e2165457ba6d105e68283bd0f
msgid "**Verify height** means that the Mission Planner will use Google Earth topology data to adjust your desired altitude at each waypoint to reflect the height of the ground beneath. So if your waypoint is on a hill, if this option is selected the *Mission Planner* will increase your ALT setting by the height of the hill. This is a good way to make sure you don't crash into mountains!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:67
#: 9f0f6472bd42494686aae9cd2fe00082
msgid "Once you are done with your mission, select **Write** and it will be sent to APM and saved in EEPROM. You can confirm that it's as you wanted by selecting **Read**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:71
#: c7b1e988b38f4cb7a35b0f3e7c1b8459
msgid "You can save multiple mission files to your local hard drive by selecting **Save WP File** or read in files with **Load WP File** in the right-click menu:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:79
#: ed4440c28b9949a7b60b5ed45eb11ae3
msgid "Tips"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:81
#: 4498cd206699480aba487bd708d94633
msgid "Prefetch: You can cache map data so you don't need Internet access at the field. Click the **Prefetch** button, and hold down **Alt** to draw a box to download the selected imagery of a location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:84
#: a3cd67f5fa2144dca822b284fb7a9bce
msgid "Grid: This allows you to draw a polygon (right click) and automatically create waypoints over the selected area. Note that it does not do \"island detection\", which means if you have a big polygon and a little one inside of that, the little one will not be excluded from the big one (see `this <http://wiki.openstreetmap.org/wiki/Relation:multipolygon>`__ for more). Also, in the case of any polygon that partially doubles backs on itself (like the letter U), the open area in the center will be included as part of the flyover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:93
#: cd5f855c1698409ab6cab09c551e1630
msgid "Setting your home location to the current location is easy, just click **Home Location** above where you enter your home location, and it will set your home location to the current coordinates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:96
#: 9c367ab15b444db8847efec4551980ed
msgid "You can measure the distance between waypoints by right-clicking at one end and selecting Measure Distance. Then right-click on the other end and select **Measure Distance** again. A dialog box will open with the distance between the two points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:100
#: 7349b1212800473cba6526d17423b4a9
msgid "The altitude change between waypoints is normally a linear ramp of target altitude as the vehicle progresses unless the altitude starts below 25m above home to prevent slow climbs potentially close to home. This value can be controlled with the :ref:`CLIMB_SLOPE_HGT<CLIMB_SLOPE_HGT>` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:105
#: 5b61770c060242b7a262b76a25618a1c
msgid "Auto grid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:107
#: 514ec429949c4d83bca290af68d1b0e4
msgid "You can also have the *Mission Planner* create a mission for you, which is useful for function like mapping missions, where the aircraft should just go back and forth in a \"lawnmower\" pattern over an area to collect photographs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:112
#: a8b86bd9a1264b38a1991b4c9abceb00
msgid "To do this, in the right-click menu select Polygon and draw a box around the area you want to map. Then select Auto WP, Grid. Follow the dialog box process to select altitude and spacing. The *Mission Planner* will then generate a mission that looks something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:120
#: 70994ad4f1964c569fc13ec1fb56aba4
msgid "Mission Planner auto-generated grid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:126
#: 30680332e1d44bbc83e0d5396647cb78
msgid "Mission commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:128
#: 1f194d3e466c493abfc484ceded9f9b6
msgid "*Mission Planner* provides a filtered list of the commands appropriate for the current vehicle type, and adds column headings for the parameters that need user-supplied values. These include navigation commands to travel to waypoints and loiter in the vicinity, DO commands to execute specific actions (for example taking pictures), and condition commands that can control when DO commands are able to run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:138
#: 51a72a325b7a474db2bbadb6fd94fd25
msgid "Example: LOITER_TURNS command withheadings for number of turns, direction, and location to loiteraround."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:140
#: 2204d84de51a430592e26212748684e6
msgid "The full set of mission commands supported by all ArduPilot platforms are listed in :ref:`MAVLink Mission Command Messages (MAV_CMD) <common-mavlink-mission-command-messages-mav_cmd>`. This includes the full name of each command (as defined in the protocol definition), information about which parameters are supported, and also the corresponding *Mission Planner* column headings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:148
#: d30a4c07681845f1bfdf3c6cececfe18
msgid "Mission Planner uses a cut-down version of the full command name. For example, commands like MAV_CMD_NAV_WAYPOINT, MAV_CMD_CONDITION_DISTANCE, MAV_CMD_DO_SET_SERVO are listed in MP as WAYPOINT, CONDITION_DISTANCE and DO_SET_SERVO respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:156
#: d76cd70779484d59a05958e84e62809a
msgid "End of Mission"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:158
#: d8d928b5898e4a429b173f60e53adc95
msgid "When the mission list is completed, Copter will loiter at the last waypoint indefinitely until a mode change or disarm (as when landing), Rover will hold, but Plane will RTL unless the last mission item is unlimited loiter or turns, or a landing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:161
#: c60c9f6daa824685aba370ddc8f79147
msgid "Mission Reset"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:163
#: 5bfa302a933e40c9b48d2fcdaa34f1ca
msgid "On Copter and Rover, the mission is reset to the beginning of the mission list every disarm. On Plane, it is reset similarly, except in AUTO mode which does not reset on disarm and leaves the \"mission pointer\" on the last mission item to be performed. For all vehicles, any landing mission item, when the landing is completed, will not advance to the next mission item. The :ref:`MIS_OPTIONS<MIS_OPTIONS>` parameter, bit 2, can be set to continue onward in the mission list after landing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:165
#: fdec542ee0bf43f094a57a6c15d81711
msgid "One can set an ``RCx_OPTION`` switch function (\"24\") to reset the mission item pointer to the beginning of the mission list at any time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-planning-a-mission-with-waypoints-and-events.rst:167
#: bc724538d959475c8bbd1e54805f6270
msgid "Also, the mission can be set to reset upon any entry into AUTO mode with the :ref:`MIS_RESTART<MIS_RESTART>` parameter. Default (0) is to resume the mission when re-entering AUTO mode."
msgstr ""

